            heat_level: null,
            standalone: true,
            audiobook: true,
            note: "The most direct ancestor of Emily Wilde's scholarly-narrator approach. Clarke frames English magic as an academic discipline with rival practitioners, footnotes, and competing theories — and then shows what happens when the actual practice turns out to be far stranger and more dangerous than the texts suggest. It is longer, drier, and considerably darker than Emily Wilde, but readers who love the idea of magic treated as a serious subject of study will find this essential. The wit is bone-dry and rewards patience.",
