            note: "Napoleonic-era military fantasy with a female soldier hiding her identity in the ranks and a colonel with a very specific and lethal history arriving to take command. Wexler shares Abercrombie's interest in competent, morally compromised soldiers operating within systems that use them as instruments — and the same honesty about what fighting actually does to the people who are good at it. The prose is cleaner and less abrasive than Abercrombie's, the politics more intricate. Readers who liked Red Country's military pragmatism without needing its frontier tone will find a solid home here.",
