        why: "The non-fantasy Western that is closest in spirit to what Red Country is doing: a cattle drive north that becomes a meditation on what the frontier actually was — exhausting, murderous, and mostly unglamorous — told through a large cast of characters Abercrombie would recognise. McMurtry's prose is warmer and more elegiac than Abercrombie's; the ending is quietly devastating in a way Red Country's is not. Required reading for anyone who responded to Red Country's genre-interrogation and wants more of the Western it is deconstructing.",
